<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls">
  <!--
    The editor's own chrome: the read-only action fields of the spec 11 panels, the lighting mode
    menu and the colour swatches.

    Each style below is a *bridge*: it points one class at a ControlTheme in
    Themes/ControlThemes/, which owns the template and the whole state matrix. Nothing here names a
    template part any more — see Themes/ControlThemes/Shared.axaml for why that convention is gone.
  -->

  <!-- ===== Tabs ===============================================================================
       The section strip has no class of its own: it is a real TabStrip on the `TabStrip` theme
       (Themes/ControlThemes/Tabs.axaml), which carries the fixed 38 height, the accent underline
       drawn as its own template part, and the cleared `Transitions` the design's zero-length tab
       swap needs. The view names that theme directly.

       There is still deliberately nothing here that switches a page transition off. The strip
       selects and a Panel below it toggles IsVisible, so no container in the app owns a page
       transition in the first place; Avalonia 11's TabStrip and TabControl carry no PageTransition
       property either — Carousel is the only container that does, and the app uses none. -->

  <!-- The `⌥` of the layer segments' `⌥1`–`⌥5` legends (mockup 1f). It is an `Icon` and not the
       character, for the same reason the picker's `↵` below is one: U+2325 is in neither embedded
       IBM Plex family, so typing it would fall through to a platform substitute and to tofu where
       there is none.

       The class is not optional. `KbdChip` sets `Foreground` — and a second one under `:disabled` —
       but an `Icon` paints from `Stroke`/`Fill` and not from `TextElement.Foreground`, so neither
       of them reaches the mark. `TextSecondary` is the chip's own rest foreground, re-spelled here
       because that is the only way it arrives.

       `Size` 12 rather than the icon law's 16: the chip's type is 10 px mono inside 6,3 of padding,
       and a 16 px mark beside it would be the tallest thing in a 38 px tab bar. `Icon.assignHint`
       set that precedent at the same number; both are recorded in docs/app/design-system.md. -->
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.kbdMark">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Size" Value="12" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== The key inspector rail ============================================================
       The Layout tab's third column (mockups 1e/2a): the inset surface, hairlined off the canvas
       beside it.

       IT CARRIES NO WIDTH ANY MORE (issue #119). The rail is drag-adjustable, and a GridSplitter
       resizes a grid COLUMN — so the width moved onto the column in KeyboardEditorView.axaml, where
       the splitter can reach it. It had to MOVE rather than be layered over: a local Width on the
       Border would have beaten whatever the column said, and the two would have disagreed the
       moment the seam was dragged. The `.wide` companion went with it; the 300px macro variant is a
       FLOOR the editor folds into EffectiveInspectorRailWidth now, not an override, so a user who
       dragged the rail past 300 is never yanked back to it.

       LightingModeRailView is the class's second consumer and sets its own local Width, which is
       still correct: that rail is not resizable and is not in this grid at all.

       The rail is NOT a modal surface and carries none of the overlay roles: the board stays
       clickable, which is the whole reason it replaces the feature panels it replaces. It is a
       column of the content grid, nothing more. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.inspectorRail">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1,0,0,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The seam between the board and the rail (issue #119) — the app's one resizable edge. A
       one-setter bridge and nothing else: the template, the hit width, the hover ramp and the focus
       ring are all the `RailSplitter` ControlTheme's, and anything set here would outrank it. -->
  <Style Selector="GridSplitter.railSplitter">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource RailSplitter}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== The hint block ====================================================================
       A neutral frame for a sentence the app says about how something works — today the rail's
       exclusivity line (issue #119), which as bare prose between the mode tabs and the amber
       mode-switch warning read as stray text and merged with the warning below it.

       DELIBERATELY NOT AMBER. Amber is the advisory colour in the design's four-status vocabulary,
       and this block warns of nothing; it is the inset surface with the same hairline every other
       block of the rail is separated by. The amber warning that can appear directly beneath it is
       unchanged, so the two are visibly different things. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.hintBlock">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,7" />
  </Style>

  <!-- One block of the rail, separated from the next by a hairline rather than by a gap: the rail
       is 268 wide and a stack of bordered cards inside it would spend a quarter of that on frames.
       `PaddingInspectorSection` is the design's own inspector rhythm. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.inspectorSection">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,1" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The footer is the same block with its hairline on top: it is pinned to the bottom of the
       rail, so the line belongs above it and not below the section it happens to follow. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.inspectorFooter">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,1,0,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The 32px tile at the head of the rail carrying the cap's own legend, so the inspector and
       the key it is talking about are visibly the same thing. Accent, because "selection" is one
       of the accent's two sanctioned meanings — this IS the selected key. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.inspectorKeyTile">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="32" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="32" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentSelectionFillBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Action fields =====================================================================
       A read-only field showing what the next captured keystroke (or the Search Keys picker) put
       there. `TokenField` targets a Border and not a TextBox, and that is the point: focus in a
       TextBox auto-suspends capture, so a focusable token field would swallow the very keypress it
       exists to record. The theme declares `.armed` itself — the advisory border a listening key
       wears — so this bridge sets nothing but the theme, or it would outrank it. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.actionField">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource TokenField}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== The token picker ==================================================================
       The Remap panel's search field, chips and result rows (mockups 1e/2a), and the same picker
       inside the macro-insertion modal. The catalog rows themselves wear the `SelectableListRow`
       control theme, named directly by the view; what is left here is the chrome around them.

       Nothing below carries a colour literal — every brush is a token — and nothing names a template
       part: the picker is authored out of a Border, a TextBox, a WrapPanel of Buttons and an Icon,
       each of which reaches its own ControlTheme through a class. -->

  <!-- The `●` of the Record button. It is an Ellipse and not the mockup's U+25CF, for the same
       reason mockup 1e's `⌕` became a Path: neither embedded IBM Plex family carries the codepoint,
       so the character would fall through to a platform substitute and tofu where there is none.
       StatusError, which handoff.md:82 names as "also the Record ● color". -->
  <Style Selector="Ellipse.recordDot">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="8" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="8" />
    <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource StatusErrorBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The same dot ON A BUTTON THAT IS NOT RED (issue #146): the macro composer's `Record key`,
       which is a `secondary` and not a `recordAction`, because the panel already carries one red
       Record — the header's `Record sequence` — and two of them would say the sequence and the
       single step were the same weight of act.

       The dot is muted while the arm is idle and goes red by DROPPING this class the moment the
       SingleStep arm goes live, which is also when the 1.4s pulse starts. So red still means "this
       is recording now" everywhere in the app, and a standing grey dot is only the affordance
       saying where recording would start. -->
  <Style Selector="Ellipse.recordDot.standby">
    <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource TextBodyMutedBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- `Ellipse.macroSlotDot` and `.macroSlotDot.filled` were deleted with issue #146. They were the
       rail's slot strip (#137) — hollow for an empty slot, filled for an occupied one — and the
       strip is a row of `Button.macroChip` now: the chip carries the slot's NUMBER, so "occupied"
       is said by the caption rather than by a fill, and "which one am I editing" by the accent ring
       rather than by a drop-down beside it. Nothing draws a dot any more, so the classes went with
       their one call site. -->

  <!-- The `↵` of the selected row's `↵ assign` hint — drawn, not typed, for the same reason. It is
       TextSecondary rather than the accent: the row it sits in is already filled with the 14%
       accent selection tint, and AccentText is the colour that rides a SOLID accent fill (white on
       the light variant), which on a tint is invisible. -->
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.assignHint">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Size" Value="12" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The result list is bounded rather than free: a dialect's catalog runs to a couple of hundred
       rows, and an unbounded list would push the picker's own chips and counter out of the rail's
       scroll long before the user reached the end of it. The pre-redesign picker capped its list the
       same way, at the same order of height.

       SINCE #133 THIS HEIGHT IS ALSO THE VIRTUALIZING VIEWPORT. The list inside is one flat
       VirtualizingStackPanel over TokenPickerViewModel.Items, and a virtualizing panel realizes what
       its effective viewport can show — which is this ScrollViewer's arranged height, because the
       presenter clips to it. Everything above it in Views/TokenPickerView.axaml measures with
       infinite height (a vertical StackPanel does), so THIS SETTER IS THE ONLY THING BOUNDING THE
       LIST: remove the cap, or let something between the two grow unbounded, and all ~200 rows
       realize again. It stays a MaxHeight rather than a Height so a short result set still collapses
       to its own size. -->
  <Style Selector="ScrollViewer.tokenPickerResults">
    <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="300" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The picker's inline advisory: amber, non-blocking, and the same tint block the rail states its
       mode-switch warning in, so "the app noticed something" reads the same wherever it is said. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.pickerAdvisory">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,7" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== The macro chips ===================================================================
       One class for the Macro panel's three rows of small latches — the SLOTS strip's numbered
       chips, the TRIGGER strip's co-triggers and the composer's four modifier latches — because the
       mock draws all three identically and they mean the same kind of thing.

       A bridge and nothing else: `MacroChip` in Themes/ControlThemes/Pills.axaml owns the face, the
       34x30 box, the `.selected` accent ring and the `.colliding` amber one, and a setter here would
       outrank it. It is NOT `toggleSegment`, which the composer's direction and delay strips keep —
       that theme fills its on-state with the full accent, and this one rings it; the theme's own
       header says why. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.macroChip">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource MacroChip}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== The macro step editor =============================================================
       The rows of the `Sequence` list, inside the 440px rail. Each row is a Button on the
       `RowButton` control theme (so hover and press are the theme's, not this file's) carrying its
       token chip, what it does and the delay behind it.

       NOTHING HERE IS A GLYPH. The drag grip is `IconDragHandle` and the insert affordance
       `IconInsert`, both drawn by the Icon control: U+283F and U+FF0B are in neither embedded IBM
       Plex family, so the mock's own characters would tofu. The `×` on the delete button IS a
       character — U+00D7 is Latin-1 and both families carry it. -->

  <!-- The grip. TextMuted at rest, because it is an affordance rather than content; it is a FILL
       mark (six dots), so the brush lands on Fill and never on Stroke. -->
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.dragHandle">
    <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource TextMutedBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Size" Value="12" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The `＋` of the trailing "insert step" row. Accent, because it is the one action in the list
       that adds something; stroke only, or the cross would fill into a square. -->
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.insertMark">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Size" Value="12" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The step list is BOUNDED (issue #139), the same way and for the same kind of reason
       `ScrollViewer.tokenPickerResults` above is: a macro runs to hundreds of keystrokes (06 §6),
       and an unbounded list pushed everything under it out of the rail's own scroll. It got worse
       when the composer became always-visible — the one control that can edit a step was below
       forty rows of list, so a long macro made its own steps uneditable.

       IT IS A FIXED `Height` SINCE ISSUE #146, not the MaxHeight it was, and the difference is the
       whole point of the mock's standing compose bar: the composer is PINNED under the list and
       must not move. With a MaxHeight the list is as tall as its contents, so recording a step,
       deleting one or landing on an empty macro slid the composer — the modifier latches, the key
       field and `Record key` — up or down the rail under the pointer, between one click and the
       next. A fixed box costs an empty gap under a two-step macro and buys a composer that is
       always in the same place.

       280 is eight rows at the row's own 6,4 rhythm. Under a 440px rail that leaves the composer,
       the Speed/Repeat block and the footer on screen together at the 680px default window. -->
  <Style Selector="ScrollViewer.macroStepList">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="280" />
  </Style>

  <!-- One step row. It is a Button so the whole row is the hit target for selecting the step that
       ⌥↑↓ moves; the theme is RowButton, which carries its hover and press.

       IT NO LONGER WEARS `.selected` (issue #146). The selection ring has to span the grip and the
       `×` as well as the row body — the mock rings the whole row — and those two sit OUTSIDE this
       button, because a button may not nest inside another button. So `Border.macroStepRowFrame`
       below wraps all three and carries the ring, and the row keeps only the hit target and the
       hover. `RowButton`'s own `.selected` face is untouched and still serves `Classes="rowButton"`
       elsewhere; nothing here overrides it, because a setter that fought a control theme would be
       the very thing this layer exists to stop. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.macroStepRow">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource RowButton}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,4" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The ring around a whole step row: grip, row body and delete mark alike. Transparent at rest
       and present-but-transparent rather than absent, so acquiring the ring on selection costs no
       reflow and the rows do not shift by a pixel as the selection moves down the list.

       AccentSelectionFill inside an AccentBrush hairline is the app's existing "this is the one you
       are editing" (Border.inspectorKeyTile says it about the selected key), which is what keeps
       the step list's selection and the rail's header reading as one statement. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.macroStepRowFrame">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="Border.macroStepRowFrame.selected">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentSelectionFillBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The key a step strikes, as a chip: the modifier marks and the token in one bordered box
       (`⌃2`), so a chord reads as one keystroke rather than as three words in a row.

       `MacroStepKey` is the app's purple for exactly this (docs/app/design-system.md), and it is set
       here as TextElement.Foreground ON THE CHIP rather than as a colour on either run inside it.
       That is trap 4 of this panel: `.keySymbol` deliberately carries no colour and a `muted` run
       beside it would have to be overridden per state — an INHERITED foreground on the container
       reaches both runs, and only one place says what colour a struck key is.

       Hidden entirely on a delay-only row (`HasToken` false), which is why the chip is a Border in
       the row rather than a border on the row: there is nothing to draw a box around there. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.macroStepToken">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource MacroStepKeyBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,2" />
    <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource MacroStepKeyBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The `80 ms` pill at the right of a step that waits. Accent, because a delay is something the
       user PUT there — "you changed this" is one of the accent's two sanctioned meanings — and the
       row's other two runs are the key (purple) and the action word (body), so the third role is
       already spoken for. A row with no delay draws an em dash in `meta muted` instead and never
       this pill: an empty accent box would be a statement about nothing.

       `AccentBrush` and NOT `AccentText` for the type, which is the distinction the token registry
       spells out and this file already leans on once (`Icon.assignHint` above): AccentText is the
       colour that rides a SOLID accent fill — near-black on dark, white on light — and this pill has
       no fill at all, so it would be invisible in one variant and near-invisible in the other. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.macroStepDelay">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,2" />
    <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- `Border.macroDelayEditor` was deleted with issue #139. It framed the per-row delay editor —
       the panel's third way to change a macro — and the composer's own `THEN WAIT` row is the only
       delay surface now, drawn inline in the composer's `inspectorSection` rather than in a block
       of its own. Nothing else reached for it, so the class went with its one call site rather than
       joining `modeOption` in the orphaned column. -->

  <!-- `Border.macroSlotCard` was deleted with issue #140. It framed one slot card of the deleted
       macro section's left half, which was its only call site, so the class went with the view. The
       rail's own slot strip — a DIFFERENT thing, and the two are easy to confuse — is
       `Button.macroChip` above since issue #146. -->

  <!-- ===== The macro composer ================================================================
       The `COMPOSE A STEP` block, pinned under the step list (issue #146). It is a bordered box
       rather than another hairlined section of the rail, and that is what the mock's own title is
       about: everything above it EDITS the sequence and everything inside it BUILDS one step, so
       the block has to read as a tool sitting on the panel rather than as the next paragraph of it.

       SurfaceInset inside a SurfaceLine hairline is the same "quiet interior" the token picker's
       result frame and `Border.hintBlock` use; PaddingCard's 14 would be too generous for a box
       already inside the section's own 12, so it carries the 12 step of the scale — spelled as a
       literal because `Space12` is an x:Double and markup cannot compose a Thickness out of one
       (the argument Themes/Geometry.axaml already makes for the card grid's three gap keys). -->
  <Style Selector="Border.composerBox">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The composer's key readout — the field that shows which key the step strikes.

       IT IS NOT AN `actionField`, and the difference is the point. A TokenField records the next
       keypress when it is clicked and goes amber while it is armed; this one only REPORTS what the
       step already holds, because arming is `Record key` beside it. Wearing the armed field's face
       would promise a click that does nothing.

       SurfaceLineHigh rather than SurfaceLine: it sits on the composer's own inset fill, where the
       plain hairline all but disappears. MinHeight 32 is the TextBox step (MonoValueField's), so the
       readout and the millisecond box on the row below line up rather than missing by two pixels. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.macroComposerKey">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The `− 1 +` group of the Repeat row: one bordered box holding two `Button.ghost` around the
       value, so the three read as one control rather than as two buttons that happen to flank a
       number.

       It replaced a Slider, which is why it is a group and not a spin box: repeat is an integer in
       a small device range (06 §6), and a slider could neither be read exactly nor stepped by one.
       The captions are the arithmetic signs `−` and `+` at the call site rather than icons — they
       are characters both embedded families carry, and a drawn mark would need two more entries in
       the icon catalog to say what U+2212 already says. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.numericStepper">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Lighting mode menu ================================================================
       One full-width row per mode, the selected one accent-filled. `ModeOption` carries the rail's
       own 9x7 rhythm and its `.selected` face. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.modeOption">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource ModeOption}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The rail row's SECOND LINE: the parameters the mode accepts ("spd · L/R", "color · spd"),
       under its name. Mockup 2f writes every row as that pair, which is what makes the rail an
       answer to "what will change if I pick this one" rather than a list of names.

       It is a descendant style and not part of the ModeOption theme because a ControlTheme's nested
       styles are matched against the templated control and its TEMPLATE children only, never
       against content the view supplied — see the theme's own header. Nothing here names a template
       part; `Button.modeOption :is(TextBlock).modeSummary` is an ordinary descendant selector.

       MUTED RATHER THAN SMALLER, which is a deviation from the mock and is recorded in
       docs/app/design-system.md: the type scale has no sans step below 11 (10 is the mono section
       label), and inventing one for a rail row would put a new number in Themes/Typography.axaml to
       separate two lines that colour already separates. The name keeps the weight step the theme
       gives the row on selection; the summary stays at 400 either way, so the pair reads as a
       heading over a caption rather than as two headings. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.modeOption :is(TextBlock).modeSummary">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextMutedBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Normal" />
  </Style>

  <!-- On the chosen row the fill is the full accent, and TextMuted on it is unreadable. AccentText
       is the colour that rides a solid accent face; the summary keeps its 400 against the name's
       500, which is what still tells the two lines apart there. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.modeOption.selected :is(TextBlock).modeSummary">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AccentTextBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== The lighting parameter row ========================================================
       The direction arrows and the speed bars under the mode rail (mockup 2f: "Color `57C4D8`",
       "Speed 6 / 9", "Direction ← → ↑ ↓"). Both are bridges and nothing else — DirectionSegment
       carries the struck-through `.unavailable` state and SpeedBar the accent fill, each in
       Themes/ControlThemes/Segmented.axaml, and a setter here would outrank them. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.directionSegment">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource DirectionSegment}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.speedBar">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource SpeedBar}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The arrow inside a direction segment, for the reason `Icon.modeMark` below exists: an Icon
       paints from a brush property and cannot inherit the button's Foreground, so the three roles
       the segment walks through are spelled out here. They are descendant selectors off the
       button's own classes rather than classes on the Icon, so a view writes the state once.

       `.unavailable` is declared LAST and therefore wins over `.selected`, which matches the
       theme's own `:not(.unavailable)` qualifier on the accent face: a struck arrow is never drawn
       as the chosen one. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.directionSegment controls|Icon">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.directionSegment.selected controls|Icon">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource AccentTextBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.directionSegment.unavailable controls|Icon">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The mark beside a mode's caption. It cannot inherit the row's foreground the way the caption
       does - an Icon paints from a brush property, not from TextElement.Foreground - so the pair of
       roles the row switches between is spelled out here instead of at the call site.

       `.filled` is the same mark drawn as a solid: three of the sixteen modes are, and an Icon's
       fill and stroke are different properties, so the class picks which one the brush lands on.
       Setting both would turn every ring in the set into a disc. -->
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.modeMark">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.modeMark.filled">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.modeMark.selected">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource AccentTextBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="controls|Icon.modeMark.filled.selected">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource AccentTextBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Colour swatches ===================================================================
       The premixed and custom squares of the picker, and the fill inside the effect/base colour
       buttons. The fill's outline is what keeps a white or a black square visible; its Background
       is device LED data, so it is the one place a colour legitimately arrives as a hex string
       from a config file rather than as a token. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.colorSwatch">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,4,4" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.colorSwatchFill">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Which of the effect / base colour buttons the picker is editing reads as an accent ring. It
       is a neutral bordered button until then, so it rides `SecondaryButton`; the ring is set on
       the Button itself, which the theme's own template binds through — the whole reason the
       presenter no longer has to be named. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.colorSlot">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.colorSlot.selected">
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
  </Style>
</Styles>
